    "chunk_size_bytes": 1601,
    "chunk_text": "Though inflation is under control, the GDP is improving and\u00a0 macro-economics are stable, the real incomes, particularly of the fixed salary earners and daily wage earners in relation to cost of living have dwindled. The lower middle class is teetering on the brink of poverty. Many have fallen. Middle class has shrunk significantly. Though unemployment has improved, the benefits of employment are meagre and insufficient to meet the basic necessities. Even the employed may be forced to look for sources of additional income. When options are limited, they may be tempted to try their hand in crime accessible to them, like bribery, cybercrime, \u00a0prostitution, and even narcotics which are pervasive and readily available. There are rumours that university students are involved in drug trafficking and even prostitution.\n\nResearch in Sri Lanka shows that there is a strong correlation between crime, literacy and poverty. It has been reported that the frequency of those sentenced to death is higher in the lower income categories and rate of conviction is high among those with low literacy and no schooling (Ekanayake, 2018). Individuals forced into crime in Sri Lanka largely emerge from deep-seated socio-economic vulnerabilities, primarily extreme poverty, low educational attainment, and youth unemployment. Marginalised communities and individuals lured by transnational job scams are frequently coerced into illicit activities due to desperate financial need and a lack of viable economic alternatives. The social and economic drivers of this phenomenon manifest in several distinct areas."
