    "num": "islandlk-9415d823",
    "date_str": "2026-07-18",
    "description": "Sumith Liyanage \u2013 boxing legend of Olympic fame",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/sumith-liyanage-boxing-legend-of-olympic-fame/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1784369880.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-18-islandlk-9415d823-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "239ffcdb9e80cfd8db0322599d3d27c9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1850,
    "chunk_text": "Sumith Liyanage \u2013 boxing legend of Olympic fame\n\nLegend is a label which is offered too generously in our country. This may be true even of \u201cnational honours\u201d. Certain characteristics or elements help define or identify a legend. Achievements of an outstanding nature in a particular trade or discipline, accomplishments beyond the reach of others and established records that can be an inspiration for emulation, stand out as elements necessary for a person to be considered a legend.\n\nSuch achievements should also have a beneficial and salutary impact on the progress of the particular discipline, the achiever becoming a catalyst for growth and progress. Legends are generally limited in number and are an exclusive or elite group.\n\nSumith Liyanage, an unbeaten national champion and competitor in boxing at the Rome Olympics in 1960 amply fits the \u2018legend\u2019 label. He retired from the police as a DIG after an unblemished career. Dignity and decorum were features of his way of life. Sumith is now 90 years old and his two colleagues from the police boxing, C P Jayasuriya and Dharmasiri Weerakoon, both legends of Olympics fame, have departed from this world. Sumith is the sole surviving Olympics boxer in the country today.\n\nHis boxing career spanned a period of 12 years. From his schooldays in Ananda and Nalanda Colleges to the Rome Olympics, the pinnacle of his career, he trod a path laden with success, glory, and impregnability. He was only once beaten here at home.\n\nHandsome, stylish, swift footed and technical, imperious, his vicious right hook floored many an opponent. In fact, this right hook floored Jercz Adamski of Poland, the reigning European champion in the \u201cFeather Weight class\u201d, at the Rome Olympics in 1960. Adamski who bounced back beat Sumith on points and eventually won the Silver Medal in his weight."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-18-islandlk-9415d823",
    "num": "islandlk-9415d823",
    "date_str": "2026-07-18",
    "description": "Sumith Liyanage \u2013 boxing legend of Olympic fame",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/sumith-liyanage-boxing-legend-of-olympic-fame/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1784369880.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-18-islandlk-9415d823-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ed41c272cacf535fd336d68d503af76e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1829,
    "chunk_text": "Handsome, stylish, swift footed and technical, imperious, his vicious right hook floored many an opponent. In fact, this right hook floored Jercz Adamski of Poland, the reigning European champion in the \u201cFeather Weight class\u201d, at the Rome Olympics in 1960. Adamski who bounced back beat Sumith on points and eventually won the Silver Medal in his weight.\n\nSomewhere in 1962, after playing for University in a Sara Trophy cricket match on the University grounds, I hurriedly made my way to the Police Depot ring (now Field Force Headquarters) to watch the Boxing Nationals. This was my first glimpse of Sumith Liyanage who tamed Corporal Wickramapala of the Army, a reputed boxer of the day, with consummate ease.\n\nHe made a distinct impression with his science, nimble footwork, speed and style. His counter punching against a bold, relentless opponent was a feature to watch. I left the venue fully satisfied after watching the amazing standards in boxing on display. I have seen some competitions in seventies and eighties but hold the view that the standards in the sixties were far superior.\n\nI remember with nostalgia, a few other excellent boxers at this meet, mostly from the Police and Armed Services : Marshall, Hepponstall, Boustead, Hope, Percy Wijesuriya, Ekanayake brothers [Hector and Bertie], Elsworth Pereira, Dharmasiri Weerakoon and CP Jayasuriya.\n\nWhen I joined the police in 1965, CP Jayasuriya, Sumith Liyanage and Dharmasiri Weerakoon were already well settled in the Service. What was evident at the time was the fierce competition among outstanding schoolboy sportsmen to enlist in the police as Sub Inspectors.\n\nThe police in those heady days of the 1960s and seventies was a much admired service.\n\nThis heritage was influenced by exemplary leaders who generally did not succumb to unlawful dictates."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-18-islandlk-9415d823",
    "num": "islandlk-9415d823",
    "date_str": "2026-07-18",
    "description": "Sumith Liyanage \u2013 boxing legend of Olympic fame",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/sumith-liyanage-boxing-legend-of-olympic-fame/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1784369880.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-18-islandlk-9415d823-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "b33e6b798aa08cca2e185719f04fd383",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1874,
    "chunk_text": "When I joined the police in 1965, CP Jayasuriya, Sumith Liyanage and Dharmasiri Weerakoon were already well settled in the Service. What was evident at the time was the fierce competition among outstanding schoolboy sportsmen to enlist in the police as Sub Inspectors.\n\nThe police in those heady days of the 1960s and seventies was a much admired service.\n\nThis heritage was influenced by exemplary leaders who generally did not succumb to unlawful dictates.\n\nThe Police is a service that works within the public gaze. Reputations with the public and performance are therefore critical ingredients that help to foster police public relations. I am not convinced that the service exploited the reputations which talented sports stars brought to the force to better enhance its public image. They were already ambassadors of public relations at the time of their entry to the police, being popularly known in the far reaches of the country.\n\nSchemes to exploit sports stars in the police by engaging them with youth, particularly school children, may have paid dividends to the service. It is also possible that these outstanding sportsmen may not have received due recognition in the matter of postings and promotions. The absence of a \u201cHall of Fame\u201d to remember sports legends may be proof of neglect.\n\nDespite a history spanning 150 years, the service has chosen to consign it\u2019s history to the dustbin. The absence of Archives and a Hall of Fame for the likes of outstanding legends\n\nsuch as Sumith Liyanage, may be proof of the inability of the service to foster respect for their past greats.\n\nI am sure that many officers of today are ignorant of names of stalwarts such as Osmund de Silva , Eleric Abeygoonewardene, Cyril Herath [uncompromising with interference], Sidney de Zoysa [DIG], and in sports, of legends such as Sumith Liyanage and Dharmasiri Weerakoon."
  },
