    "language": "en",
    "md5": "29875cfb57a687204fc661e1f5969253",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 702,
    "chunk_text": "Worrying observations during Kataragama Pada Yatra\n\nPada Yatra is an annual pilgrimage from Nagadipa to Kataragama which is an important event mainly for Hindu devotees. Pilgrims travel via the Yala National Park prior to reaching their destination. A video that went viral recently shows pilgrims trying to avoid an elephant in close proximity. It wasn\u2019t clear whether it was the pilgrims that had blocked the elephant\u2019s path or vice versa. However, the point is that such pilgrimages should be done with much caution. At a time when the human-wildlife conflict has escalated, people should be mindful about their journey through habitats frequented by wild animals such as leopards and elephants."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-20-dailymirrorlk-5e15c289",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-5e15c289",
    "date_str": "2026-07-20",
    "description": "Mr. President, popularity must now bring results!",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Mr-President-popularity-must-now-bring-results/172-346053",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1784499003.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-20-dailymirrorlk-5e15c289-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7de17da7d026c3211b8516724d448ebc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1823,
    "chunk_text": "Mr. President, popularity must now bring results!\n\nFor most Sri Lankans, one issue matters above all others. The cost of living\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake continues to enjoy strong public support. In a country where trust in leaders has often been short lived, this is significant. According to the latest survey by the Centre for Policy Alternatives, his approval rating stands at 75.5 percent. That number reflects hope. It reflects a public that still believes change is possible.\n\nBut in Sri Lanka, popularity can fade quickly. People have seen leaders rise with promise before, only to fall when results did not follow. The real test for this administration has now begun. Support must turn into delivery.\n\nFor most Sri Lankans, one issue matters above all others. The cost of living.\n\nThere is a clear gap between talk of recovery and the reality people face each day. The economy may be stabilising on paper, but daily life is still a struggle. Prices remain high. Salaries have not kept up. Managing a household has become a constant balancing act. Basic food items still take up a large share of income. Electricity bills continue to worry families. Transport costs remain high, whether it is fuel or bus fares. School expenses add another layer of pressure.\n\nTake a young couple living in Colombo. Both may be working, yet most of their income goes towards rent, food, and bills. Saving is difficult. For a pensioner, the situation is even harder. Medical costs alone can take away much of a monthly income.\n\nThese are not isolated cases. This is the reality for a large part of the country.\n\nThis is why the government must act with urgency. People are not asking for dramatic change overnight. They are asking for relief they can actually feel. Even small reductions in key costs can make a difference."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-20-dailymirrorlk-5e15c289",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-5e15c289",
    "date_str": "2026-07-20",
    "description": "Mr. President, popularity must now bring results!",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Mr-President-popularity-must-now-bring-results/172-346053",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1784499003.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-20-dailymirrorlk-5e15c289-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "00d9e4afb291bf74d0bd1572660df46c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1705,
    "chunk_text": "This is why the government must act with urgency. People are not asking for dramatic change overnight. They are asking for relief they can actually feel. Even small reductions in key costs can make a difference.\n\nAt the same time, the country cannot only focus on managing hardship. It must create opportunity. This is where foreign direct investment becomes essential.\n\nFor years, Sri Lanka has spoken about its potential. Its location is ideal. Its people are skilled. Yet large investments have not come in the way they should due to lack of confidence. Investors look for stability. They want clear policies and quick decisions, not delays, changing rules, and long processes. If this is to change, the system must improve. Large investments will create jobs and give young people a reason to stay in the country.\n\nToday, many young Sri Lankans are still planning to leave. They are not only looking for better pay. They are looking for stability and a future they can rely on. But even the best plans will fail if they are not carried out properly. This brings us to a key challenge,\u00a0 the bureaucracy. People often feel that while decisions are made at the top, they do not move through the system.\n\nFor example, a small business owner trying to expand may wait months for approvals. An investor ready to commit funds may lose interest due to delays. A citizen seeking a simple service may have to go from office to office. The President must address this directly. It is not enough to have the right policies. They must be implemented efficiently.\n\nThere are capable people within the system. But they need clear direction and the space to act. A culture of results must replace a culture of delay."
  },
