    "chunk_text": "Hundreds of policemen had been deployed and all roads leading to the venue have been sealed to traffic. Security has been stepped up across the city with barricades on a number of main streets.\n\nPolicemen were seen checking vehicles on roads leading to the area and asking questions to drivers and passengers.\n\nHe has demanded for Wangchuk to be released from hospital so that he can join the march. Wangchuk's wife Gitanjali Angmo is expected to participate in the march.\n\nWangchuk has said he would end his fast if the government took accountability \"for the recent failures in the education system\"; if he and the CJP leadership reached parliament and MPs assured them they would take up the issue; or if lawmakers visited him in hospital and assured him this would happen.\n\nProtests have been organised in other cities as well. A massive protest was held in Mumbai city on Sunday while another one has been planned on Monday. Protests were also held in Bengaluru and Hyderabad.\n\nWangchuk's fast in the scorching Delhi summer had drawn a lot of attention even before he was forcibly removed. He had been consuming just salt and water and had lost more than 9kg (1.4 stone). Despite the discomfort he had vowed to take part in the march to parliament.\n\nBut on Saturday, chaos erupted just before 07:30 local time (02:00 GMT) when dozens of policemen covered him with curtains of bedsheets before removing him from the stage.\n\nThe CJP's protests have centred around cancellation of several important exams following allegations of paper leaks.\n\nIn early May, a crucial medical entrance exam was cancelled after a paper leak, affecting nearly 2.28 million candidates who wrote the test. A retest was held on 21 June - but more than 20 students died by suicide, according to their families."
