    "md5": "20e53e2b3a7d3cf596e116af1e721856",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1621,
    "chunk_text": "CSE opens the week lower as broad-based selling offsets strong institutional activity\n\nThe Colombo Stock Exchange commenced the week on a negative note, with both benchmark indices declining steadily throughout Monday's trading session as broad-based selling pressure continued to weigh on investor sentiment. Although market turnover and share volume improved compared to the previous session, the increase was primarily driven by crossing transactions rather than widespread buying interest, highlighting the cautious stance adopted by market participants.\n\nRetail participation remained subdued as investors continued to await stronger earnings-driven catalysts, while significant crossing activity indicated that institutional and value-oriented investors remained active in selectively accumulating fundamentally strong counters. Despite this institutional participation, persistent selling pressure across the broader market kept both indices firmly in negative territory by the close.\n\nThe ASPI declined by 197.31 points, or 0.92%, to close at 21,208.10, while the S&P SL20 Index fell by 56.46 points, or 0.94%, to settle at 5,943.22. Total market turnover amounted to LKR 2.63 Bn, with 66.49 Mn shares traded.\n\nThe Food Retailing sector emerged as the largest contributor to market turnover, generating LKR 1.14 Bn with 2.28 Mn shares traded. CARG.N led the sector, recording LKR 0.58 Bn in turnover on 0.91 Mn shares traded. Crossing transactions accounted for LKR 1.35 Bn, representing 51% of total market turnover, with the largest crossing recorded in CTHR.N amounting to LKR 0.56 Bn involving 1.10 Mn shares."
  },
