    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6ef0ab0250765435263aadcee12a7f5f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1788,
    "chunk_text": "Domestically, the gazette also outlines notable increases for corporate structures across the board. The baseline registration for a private limited company has seen a modest rise to Rs.5,200. However, incorporating a public limited company now requires Rs.26,400.\n\nFor the re-registration of the existing companies, excluding the foreign entities, the fee has been revised to Rs.13,200, while the Gamidiriya companies can re-register as public companies for Rs.3,900.\n\nThe revised framework introduces comprehensive hikes for professional service providers dealing with corporate compliance. The registration fee for individual company secretaries has increased to Rs.6,600, while sole proprietorships, partnerships or limited companies acting as secretaries will now face a unified fee of Rs.13,200.\n\nSimilarly, the auditing professionals will encounter steeper costs. Registering as an individual auditor is now priced at Rs.7,900, with auditor partnerships subject to a Rs.19,800 fee. The annual renewal of these auditing registrations has been updated to Rs.5,200 for individuals and Rs.7,900 for firms.\n\nRoutine administrative tasks and public access to company records have not been spared in the broad regulatory update. The preliminary step of securing a company name approval now commands a fee of Rs.2,600, while the filing of a mandatory annual return is set at Rs.7,900.\n\nEstablishing collateral lines has also become costlier, with the registration of mortgages, charges and debentures fixed at Rs.9,900. Additionally, accessing corporate transparency features has seen a uniform pricing adjustment; inspecting a file kept by the Registrar General of Companies, inspecting the register of charges or obtaining a certification of any document extract will each cost Rs.1,300."
  },
