    "chunk_size_bytes": 1822,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lankan war heroes risk being arrested and imprisoned in foreign countries: Joint opposition\n\nColombo, July 21 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka's military leaders risk being arrested and tried, in terms of the principle of universal jurisdiction, in the national courts of different countries as a result of the investigative mechanism established by the Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights, the Joint Opposition raised alarm yesterday.\n\nMaking remarks during the weekly press conference, its convener, Prof. G.L. Peiris said yesterday that officials of this special investigative body had visited multiple countries and gathered evidence against Sri Lanka's military leaders over serious allegations. They are now processing such evidence, according to him.\n\n\"They have been able to gather 96,215 pieces of evidence from 470 sources. According to them, there are 220 witnesses and 250 institutions,\" he said.\n\nAgainst the backdrop, he said, Sri Lankan military leaders stand the risk of being arrested, tried and imprisoned in different countries whenever they travel abroad for a holiday, medical purposes, or to attend to the educational needs of their children. He said the countries concerned would invoke the principle of universal jurisdiction.\n\nHe said the previous governments, which he represented, flatly rejected the involvement of foreign countries in hearing cases of Sri Lankans in this manner. He said that the then government ensured an internationally acclaimed judiciary in Sri Lanka.\n\n\"It was a source of strength for us. As such, we argued against the involvement of foreign courts,\" he said.\n\nReferring to the government's plan for the extension of the retirement age of judges, he said it had been a move widely opposed by civil society, the Sri Lanka Bar Association, academia and others."
