    "md5": "d0b96abbdb5bb23687109cf9493aad3d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1777,
    "chunk_text": "Cleanliness is a public responsibility too!\n\nSri Lanka is once again in the grip of dengue. But this time, it is no longer just another seasonal warning. It has crossed into something far more serious.\n\nBy July 2026, more than 50 lives have already been lost and tens of thousands infected. The numbers continue to rise, particularly in high-density districts such as Colombo, Gampaha and Kalutara. Hospitals are under pressure. And yet, the most troubling part is not the scale of the outbreak. It is the fact that we have seen this all before.\n\nDengue is not new to Sri Lanka. It has been part of our public health reality for decades. We know how it spreads. We know that the Aedes mosquito breeds in stagnant water. We know that poor waste management, blocked drains and neglected spaces create the perfect conditions for its spread.\n\nThere is, of course, a responsibility that lies with the state. Local councils in particular must take a hard look at their performance. Garbage piled up for days. Drains clogged with plastic and debris. Water collecting in abandoned lands. Construction sites left unchecked. They are part of daily life.\n\nAnd this is where the problem begins. Local authorities cannot continue to operate in a reactive manner. Every year, we see the same pattern. When cases rise, there is urgency. But once the immediate threat fades, so does the effort.\n\nDengue control cannot be seasonal. It must be constant. Drains need to be cleaned before the rains begin. Waste must be collected on time. High-risk areas must be monitored regularly. There must also be accountability. When a particular area reports a spike in cases, the relevant local council should be able to explain why. But if we are honest, the blame does not lie only with the authorities."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
