    "chunk_id": "2026-07-22-dailyftlk-cfb8db3a-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e92fc0408938b7f2d6df13159fac6792",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2023,
    "chunk_text": "Efficient public sector, accountable management accounting vital for sustainable development: PM\n\nCMA Sri Lanka Founder and President Prof. Lakshman\u00a0R. Watawala addressing the inaugural session\n\nPrime Minister Dr. Harini Amarasuriya said an efficient public sector supported by an accountable management accounting system is essential to secure Sri Lanka\u2019s sustainable development as the country seeks to consolidate its recovery from the economic crisis.\n\nAddressing the inaugural session of the CMA Sri Lanka National Management Accounting Conference 2026 in Colombo on Tuesday, the Prime Minister said sound management accounting had become a strategic necessity for both the public and private sectors amid growing global uncertainty.\n\n\u201cAs Sri Lanka continues its recovery from the economic crisis, an efficient public sector together with an accountable management accounting system is essential to ensure the country\u2019s sustainable development,\u201d she said.\n\nDr. Amarasuriya said management accounting now extends well beyond financial reporting and plays a central role in supporting strategic decision-making.\n\n\u201cIn the face of today\u2019s global uncertainty, management accounting is far more than financial reporting. It is a vital tool that supports sound strategic decision-making in both the public and private sectors.\u201d\n\nShe said Sri Lanka was steadily emerging from its financial crisis following the sacrifices made by the people and policy decisions taken by the Government, noting progress in the manufacturing, tourism and financial sectors.\n\n\u201cThe challenge before us now is to further consolidate economic stability while building a competitive and resilient economy,\u201d she said.\n\nHighlighting the importance of accountability in the public sector, the Prime Minister said several Government institutions and ministries had failed to submit annual financial statements and audit reports over the past several years, but the present Government had taken steps to update the outstanding reports."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
