    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e79e9a216ae8a97b4ddc153d9e8b5c08",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1792,
    "chunk_text": "Highlighting the importance of accountability in the public sector, the Prime Minister said several Government institutions and ministries had failed to submit annual financial statements and audit reports over the past several years, but the present Government had taken steps to update the outstanding reports.\n\nDr. Amarasuriya also called for greater reliance on evidence and data in public administration, saying policy decisions should not be influenced by personal agendas.\n\n\u201cDecisions within the public administration must not be driven by personal agendas but by evidence, data, and scientific analysis.\u201d\n\nShe said accounting frameworks should also evolve to reflect changing global priorities by recognising environmental degradation and social costs that have traditionally received limited attention.\n\nReferring to the Government\u2019s education reforms, the Prime Minister said vocational training Programs and a one-year institutional internship would be incorporated into the Advanced Level curriculum, adding that the Government looked forward to the support of recognised professional institutions in implementing the initiative.\n\nShe also acknowledged the contribution of professional bodies, including CMA Sri Lanka, in developing professionals with the ethical values and strategic thinking required in an economy increasingly shaped by digital technology and artificial intelligence.\n\nDuring the opening ceremony, Dr. Amarasuriya launched the CMA National Management Accounting Conference Journal and presented Golden Jubilee Awards to senior professionals in recognition of 50 years of service to the accounting profession.\n\nCMA Sri Lanka also awarded 100 full scholarships to the Government for public sector officers to pursue the CMA Management Accounting Programme."
  },
