    "chunk_size_bytes": 1672,
    "chunk_text": "When ports become pawns: How the US is reanchoring its maritime strategy in South Asia\n\nThe Indian Ocean Region has historically functioned as the central, beating artery of global commerce, accommodating over one-third of the world's bulk cargo and two-thirds of global seaborne oil shipments. While South Asian states have long viewed their maritime gateways primarily as engines for economic growth, these commercial ports are undergoing a volatile transformation. Driven by the intensifying great-power competition between the United States and China, commercial ports are increasingly functioning as geopolitical pawns in a high-stakes contest for maritime supremacy and supply chain resilience.\n\nFaced with the sprawling infrastructure of China\u2019s Belt and Road Initiative (BRI) and kinetic threats from an increasingly unstable Middle East, the United States is fundamentally recalibrating its maritime strategy in South Asia. Washington is transitioning away from a Cold War-era posture of unilateral dominance anchored by isolated military bases, moving instead toward a complex strategy of diffusion, economic statecraft, and asymmetric minilateralism.\n\nFor decades, U.S. maritime dominance in the Indian Ocean relied heavily on its fortified base at Diego Garcia. However, China\u2019s expanding naval capabilities and growing presence through strategic port investments, combined with emerging uncertainties over the Chagos Archipelago, have encouraged Washington to diversify its regional approach. The 2026 conflict involving the United States, Israel, and Iran further highlighted the vulnerabilities of relying on a small number of fixed military facilities."
