    "chunk_size_bytes": 1874,
    "chunk_text": "The Port of Colombo is South Asia\u2019s largest transshipment hub and ranks among the world\u2019s leading container ports. Operating above 90 per cent capacity since 2021, its strategic location along the main east-west shipping route between Asia, Europe, and the Americas makes it a vital regional maritime asset. The port is also crucial for India, handling around 45 per cent of its container transshipment traffic.\n\nAs trade volumes continue to expand, Colombo's importance has grown beyond its commercial role, placing it at the centre of increasing geopolitical competition.\n\nWithin US policy circles, Colombo is increasingly recognised as a strategically important logistics hub in the Indo-Pacific. Washington's engagement is aimed at promoting what it describes as transparent and high-quality infrastructure partnerships as an alternative to China's growing presence across South Asia. Reflecting this growing engagement, a Sri Lankan delegation recently presented investment opportunities in Colombo Port development to the US business community during a maritime summit in Washington.\n\nParticular attention has focused on the Colombo West International Terminal (CWIT), which has become a key project attracting international investors. The terminal is designed to handle 3.2 million TEUs annually and features advanced automated operations, a 1,400-metre quay, a 20-metre draft and some of the region's tallest electric ship-to-shore cranes, enabling simultaneous handling of the world's largest container vessels.\n\nThe project is being developed through a joint venture led by India's Adani Ports, which holds a 51 per cent stake, alongside John Keells Holdings with 34 per cent and the Sri Lanka Ports Authority with 15 per cent. Strong demand has accelerated planning for the proposed West Container Terminal 2, with detailed designs already being fast-tracked."
