    "chunk_text": "For the Maldives, the priority remains transforming Thilafushi into a modern commercial port capable of supporting economic diversification and long-term growth. However, as major powers deepen their interest in strategically located infrastructure, the country's ports are becoming more than commercial assets. Like Colombo and other Indian Ocean hubs, Thilafushi is increasingly emerging as a focal point where economic development, maritime security and geopolitical competition converge, underscoring the challenge facing smaller states seeking investment while preserving their strategic autonomy.\n\nDespite the influx of investment and diplomatic attention, host nations across South Asia must remain explicit and cautious about the underlying motives of the U.S. maritime strategy. For Washington, this approach frequently prioritizes American geopolitical interests, specifically deterring adversarial influence and securing vital maritime logistics far above the immediate, pressing developmental needs of local economies. When critical infrastructure decisions are heavily influenced by the strategic objectives of a distant superpower, it fundamentally complicates sovereign decision-making. Developing nations, many of which are intensely focused on sustainable growth and debt recovery, find themselves pressured to align their commercial maritime policies with Western security agendas, risking artificial policy divisions that do little to address their actual domestic priorities."
