    "md5": "cbfb3427f621f2a6cc962b4ce4379678",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1736,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s exports surpass US$9 billion in first half of 2026\n\nColombo, July 22 (Daily Mirror) - Sri Lanka's total exports exceeded the US$9 billion mark during the first six months of 2026, reflecting continued growth in both merchandise and services exports despite challenges in several key sectors.\n\nAccording to the Sri Lanka Export Development Board (EDB), combined merchandise and services exports reached an estimated US$9.01 billion between January and June 2026, marking an 8% increase compared to the same period last year.\n\nMerchandise exports rose by 8.95% year-on-year to US$7.07 billion, while services exports increased by 4.49% to US$1.94 billion, highlighting the growing contribution of knowledge-based industries such as ICT/BPM, construction, financial services, transport and logistics.\n\nIn June alone, merchandise exports increased by 15.09% to US$1.31 billion, while services exports grew by 3.75% to US$344.52 million. As a result, total exports for the month reached US$1.66 billion, up 12.53% from June 2025.\n\nEDB Chairman and Chief Executive Officer Mangala Wijesinghe said the performance demonstrated the resilience and competitiveness of Sri Lankan exporters despite evolving global market conditions.\n\nHe said the EDB would continue working with exporters and stakeholders under the National Export Development Plan (2026\u20132030) to strengthen export competitiveness, diversify markets and promote value-added exports.\n\nAmong sectors recording strong growth in June were electrical and electronic components, food and beverages, rubber and rubber-based products, spices and essential oils, seafood, mineral products and ornamental fish. ICT/BPM services also maintained steady growth during the month."
  },
