    "chunk_text": "Behavioural patterns of mosquitoes and their impact on spread of Dengue infection\n\nSri Lanka is currently experiencing a widespread outbreak of dengue. Dengue is transmitted by mosquitoes of the genus Aedes. In Sri Lanka, Aedes aegypti, a species that thrives in urban areas, and Aedes albopictus, which primarily inhabits semi-urban or rural habitats, are the primary vectors of dengue. Aedes species are also the transmitters of chikungunya infections and yellow fever in South American countries.\n\nOne of the major efforts to control the spread of dengue is to limit or eliminate their breeding places. These mosquitoes are generally known as \u201ccontainer breeders\u201d because they prefer small water collections as found in discarded items such as tin cans, coconut shells, plastic cups, open-mouthed bottles, and discarded tyres. Indoor preferential breeding sites are ant traps, flower vases, and small water collections in traps under refrigerators, and small collections in and around bathrooms. The amount of water is not a limiting factor. Sometimes a trace of dampness is sufficient, while overhead water tanks, with a fair amount of water, are not overlooked. These mosquitoes avoid water with dead and decaying organic matter. Hence, the time and effort expended in unblocking blocked drains and such surroundings are less effective, though a healthy measure when it comes to dengue mosquito control. Water collecting in fallen leaves under trees is also not spared. Aedes albopictus prefers tree holes and leaf axils for egg laying.\n\nAedes mosquitoes are easily identified with the naked eye. Their body colour is generally black with white scales on the body, making distinctive patterns. Aedes aegypti has a lyre-shaped marking on the back of the thorax behind the head, while Aedes albopictus is distinguished by a single white line in the middle of the thorax (see figure)."
