    "md5": "bb9231f6ab3950ad146d543fd2e40c87",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1769,
    "chunk_text": "Aedes mosquitoes are easily identified with the naked eye. Their body colour is generally black with white scales on the body, making distinctive patterns. Aedes aegypti has a lyre-shaped marking on the back of the thorax behind the head, while Aedes albopictus is distinguished by a single white line in the middle of the thorax (see figure).\n\nOnly female mosquitoes bite humans and animals for blood meals, while the males subsist on plant juices. This is because the proteins in blood meals are necessary for the maturation of eggs in their ovaries. However, all female mosquitoes are not \u201cequal opportunity feeders\u201d. That means they have their preferences for victims over others. People have often wondered why only some individuals are harassed by mosquitoes while some escape the onslaught. Similarly, it is rare for two or three siblings in a family to have dengue simultaneously.\n\nAs mentioned earlier, mosquitoes are not equal-opportunity biters or feeders. They are known to be attracted to the microbiota, or the microorganisms that colonise our skin. Another factor that attracts mosquitoes is the amount of carbon dioxide emitted in the breath. This implies that individuals who engage in physical activities during the day are at a higher risk of being bitten. Pregnant women and alcohol drinkers also fall into this category.\n\nSome studies show that mosquitoes are attracted to certain colours worn by individuals \u2013 red, orange, and black being most preferred. Individual body odour has been attributed as an attractant for these mosquitoes, and some point out that the individual genetic constitution is also an important factor. A study published in 2015 found that DNA may account for nearly 67% of mosquito attraction. Involving sets of twins,"
