    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "4f7c351bbc51825b7df16be701d447fb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1937,
    "chunk_text": "How leaderless youth movements are reshaping politics in South Asia\n\nPolitical analysts attribute this regional phenomenon of youth uprisings to a shared sense of generational betrayal\n\nSri Lanka has a long and rather bloodied history of protests, uprisings, insurrections, enforced disappearances and everything in between. The ongoing excavations at the Chemmani mass grave highlight the gravity of the atrocities committed in recent history. A recently excavated skeleton, referred to as Skeleton 429 was unlike other any skeletal remains that were being unearthed. The skeleton was discovered in a tight, seated posture with knees pulled sharply towards the chest and the torso slumped forward, leaving many unanswered questions.\n\nJuly is a significant month for Sri Lanka as there are many memorials and events being organised to commemorate Black July. The violence was triggered when the Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam (LTTE) ambushed a Sri Lankan Army patrol in the Northern Province, killing 13 soldiers. This incident served as a catalyst for premeditated and widespread riots across the country.\u00a0 Long simmering tensions between the majority Sinhalese and the largely Hindu minority eventually led to the 26-year long armed conflict.\n\nThe country has come a long way since then. People have seen the best and worst of times, in the form of ethnic divisions, authoritarian rulers, political plots, people\u2019s movements in the form of Aragalaya,\u00a0 and how corrupt regimes were eventually overthrown. In fact,\u00a0 Sri Lanka inspired citizens from many other countries to realise the power they have in their hands. Gen-Z protests in Bangladesh and Nepal are examples from the South Asian region.\u00a0 The Gen-Z uprising in Kenya is another example from another part of the world. In each one of these uprisings, people and mostly the Gen-Z stood up against political corruption, police brutality, and ban on social media and so on."
  },
