    "chunk_text": "Beware of fake police officers demanding bank details on WhatsApp\n\nColombo, July 23 (Daily Mirror) - The Sri Lanka Police have issued an urgent public warning over a surge in financial scams involving fraudsters impersonating police officers and other government officials to steal money and sensitive banking information.\n\nPolice said criminals are using stolen or fabricated photographs to create fake profiles on messaging platforms such as WhatsApp and Telegram to deceive members of the public. Despite previous arrests and awareness campaigns, police said people continue to fall victim to the scams.\n\nAccording to police, victims are contacted by individuals falsely claiming to represent the Central Bank of Sri Lanka, the Criminal Investigation Department (CID), or other police investigation units. The callers typically allege that the victim's National Identity Card has been used in fraudulent activities or that their bank accounts are linked to money laundering and other criminal offences.\n\nTo make their claims appear genuine, the scammers send forged police identity cards, official-looking documents and badges through social media while threatening victims with legal action and offering to help resolve the purported investigations.\n\nPolice said the fraudsters then persuade victims to reveal confidential information, including One-Time Passwords (OTPs), banking credentials and PINs. In some cases, victims are pressured into transferring money to unfamiliar bank accounts or purchasing and transferring cryptocurrency, including USDT.\n\nThe Police stressed that no government institution or private organisation will ever request OTPs, CVV numbers, bank passwords or other confidential banking details over the telephone or through social media."
  },
