    "md5": "2ad74b37ba5a73479f04216f816e8c48",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1575,
    "chunk_text": "Aquaculture also presents enormous growth potential. Sustainable fish farming, shrimp cultivation, seaweed production, and shellfish farming can reduce pressure on wild fish populations while creating new export opportunities. Advances in environmentally friendly aquaculture technologies allow producers to increase yields while minimising impacts on coastal ecosystems. As global demand for sustainably sourced seafood continues to rise, Sri Lanka can strengthen its position in premium international markets by maintaining high environmental and quality standards.\n\nRenewable ocean energy represents an emerging frontier for sustainable development. Offshore wind energy, wave energy, and tidal power are receiving increasing global attention as countries seek to diversify energy sources and reduce dependence on fossil fuels. Although these technologies are still developing, Sri Lanka\u2019s geographical location provides opportunities for future investment in marine renewable energy projects that support national energy security and climate goals.\n\nMarine biodiversity is among Sri Lanka\u2019s greatest natural assets. Coral reefs, seagrass beds, mangroves, and coastal wetlands provide essential ecosystem services, including carbon sequestration, shoreline protection, fisheries habitats, and tourism opportunities. Unfortunately, these ecosystems face growing threats from pollution, unsustainable development, rising sea temperatures, and plastic waste. Conservation and restoration efforts must therefore become integral components of national development planning."
