    "chunk_size_bytes": 1911,
    "chunk_text": "Marine biodiversity is among Sri Lanka\u2019s greatest natural assets. Coral reefs, seagrass beds, mangroves, and coastal wetlands provide essential ecosystem services, including carbon sequestration, shoreline protection, fisheries habitats, and tourism opportunities. Unfortunately, these ecosystems face growing threats from pollution, unsustainable development, rising sea temperatures, and plastic waste. Conservation and restoration efforts must therefore become integral components of national development planning.\n\nThe Blue Economy also creates opportunities for innovation. Marine biotechnology is an emerging field exploring the use of marine organisms for pharmaceuticals, cosmetics, food products, and industrial applications. Research institutions and private companies can collaborate to develop high-value products while ensuring sustainable resource management. Investment in ocean science and marine research will be critical for unlocking these future industries.\n\nDigital technology is transforming ocean management across the world. Satellite monitoring, artificial intelligence, drones, Internet of Things (IoT) sensors, and advanced data analytics enable governments and businesses to monitor marine ecosystems, detect illegal fishing, optimise shipping routes, and improve disaster preparedness. These technologies enhance operational efficiency while strengthening environmental protection.\n\nClimate change further highlights the importance of sustainable ocean management. Rising sea levels, coastal erosion, ocean warming, and changing weather patterns threaten communities, infrastructure, fisheries, and tourism. Nature-based solutions such as mangrove restoration, coral reef conservation, and coastal ecosystem protection improve resilience while supporting biodiversity and carbon storage. Investing in these natural assets represents both an environmental and economic opportunity."
