    "chunk_size_bytes": 1740,
    "chunk_text": "Climate change further highlights the importance of sustainable ocean management. Rising sea levels, coastal erosion, ocean warming, and changing weather patterns threaten communities, infrastructure, fisheries, and tourism. Nature-based solutions such as mangrove restoration, coral reef conservation, and coastal ecosystem protection improve resilience while supporting biodiversity and carbon storage. Investing in these natural assets represents both an environmental and economic opportunity.\n\nCorporate leadership also plays an essential role. Companies operating in shipping, logistics, tourism, fisheries, manufacturing, and coastal development must integrate ESG principles into their operations by improving governance, reducing emissions, protecting biodiversity, and engaging local communities. Transparent sustainability reporting demonstrates accountability and builds confidence among investors, customers, and regulators.\n\nCollaboration between government, private sector, academia, local communities, and international partners will ultimately determine the success of Sri Lanka\u2019s Blue Economy. Integrated marine spatial planning, effective environmental regulations, investment in education and research, and public awareness programmes can help ensure that ocean resources are managed responsibly for long-term national benefit.\n\nThe transition towards a sustainable Blue Economy is not simply an environmental initiative\u2014it is a strategic economic opportunity. By protecting marine ecosystems while promoting responsible investment and innovation, Sri Lanka can strengthen food security, expand exports, create high-quality employment, attract international investment, and enhance resilience against climate change."
