    "chunk_size_bytes": 1766,
    "chunk_text": "This shift is encouraging Sri Lankan businesses to embed sustainability into their corporate strategies rather than treating it as a standalone initiative.\n\nGreen finance creates numerous opportunities for companies of every size.\n\nTechnology companies can develop innovative climate solutions, smart energy management systems, and digital ESG reporting platforms.\n\nSmall and Medium Enterprises (SMEs) can also benefit through sustainable financing programmes designed specifically to improve resource efficiency and reduce operating costs.\n\nThese investments not only support environmental objectives but frequently enhance profitability through reduced energy consumption, operational savings, and stronger customer loyalty.\n\nDespite encouraging progress, several challenges continue to limit the growth of green finance in Sri Lanka.\n\nThese include limited awareness among businesses, inconsistent sustainability reporting, insufficient technical expertise, lack of common ESG standards, higher initial investment costs, and limited availability of specialised green financial products.\n\nAddressing these challenges requires collaboration among regulators, financial institutions, businesses, universities, professional bodies, and international development partners.\n\nCapacity building, policy consistency, investor education, and stronger sustainability disclosure frameworks will help accelerate market development.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s transition towards a greener economy will require long-term commitment, strategic investment, and collective action across both the public and private sectors.\n\nGovernment policy, financial innovation, corporate leadership, and responsible consumer behaviour must work together to create an ecosystem that rewards sustainability."
