    "md5": "dbfd9dc2424c26355af3d282e5f95bc8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1763,
    "chunk_text": "What do consumers actually expect from a company facing all this? Not perfection, generally. Most people understand that global supply chains are complicated and that eliminating every problem overnight isn\u2019t realistic. What they expect instead is honesty about where problems exist and evidence of real effort to fix them. A company that admits a supplier failed an audit and explains what it\u2019s doing about it tends to earn more trust than one that claims everything is already flawless. Overpromising and getting caught out later does far more damage than admitting a difficult truth up front.\n\nMeeting these expectations requires more than a page on a company website. It means actually auditing suppliers, rather than accepting their word, and returning to check on them regularly, rather than treating one audit as a permanent stamp of approval. It means building relationships with fewer, better-vetted suppliers rather than constantly switching to whoever offers the lowest price, since price pressure is often exactly what pushes suppliers toward cutting corners on wages and safety. And it means being willing to walk away from a supplier that won\u2019t meet basic standards, even when that supplier is cheaper or more convenient.\n\nConsumers won\u2019t remain satisfied with vague promises for much longer, if they ever were. The direction of travel is toward more scrutiny, not less, helped along by regulation, journalism, and the simple fact that information about a company\u2019s practices is harder to hide than it used to be. Businesses that treat ethical sourcing as a genuine operating standard, rather than a marketing exercise, are likely to face fewer surprises and less reputational damage than those still hoping nobody looks too closely. (M P)"
