    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7d88e97dcd94ec90b0446d81f452c55b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1458,
    "chunk_text": "Why Circular Economy Practices Matter for Manufacturers\n\nFor most of the last century, manufacturing followed a simple pattern: take raw materials, make a product, sell it, and let the customer worry about what happens once it wears out. That model, often called the linear economy, worked well enough when materials were cheap, and landfill space seemed endless. Neither of those assumptions holds up anymore. Raw material prices have grown less predictable, waste disposal has become more expensive and more regulated, and customers are asking harder questions about what happens to a product after they\u2019re done with it. Manufacturers are responding by borrowing an old idea and giving it a new name: the circular economy.\n\nThe basic idea is straightforward. Instead of a straight line from raw material to landfill, a circular approach tries to keep materials in use for as long as possible, through repair, reuse, and recycling, before anything is thrown away. A washing machine designed to be taken apart and rebuilt rather than scrapped. A phone manufacturer that buys back old devices to recover valuable metals rather than mining new ones. A furniture company that leases desks and chairs to offices, then refurbishes and re-leases them once a tenant moves out. These aren\u2019t hypothetical examples; companies in each of these industries already run programs like this, and the ones doing it well have found it cuts costs as often as it cuts waste."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-23-dailyftlk-10cb02cb",
    "num": "dailyftlk-10cb02cb",
    "date_str": "2026-07-23",
    "description": "Why Circular Economy Practices Matter for Manufacturers",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/business/Why-Circular-Economy-Practices-Matter-for-Manufacturers/34-795037",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
