    "md5": "102bfd05e9865300df2c5aa1279ab73e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1978,
    "chunk_text": "This broader understanding is reflected in initiatives such as CICT\u2019s long-standing support for Sri Lanka\u2019s Wheelchair Tennis programme, which has helped athletes compete internationally while promoting inclusion through sport, and the Ambassador Cup, in partnership with the University of Colombo, which brings together students from universities across Sri Lanka through badminton, encouraging positive engagement and helping foster a more inclusive and constructive university culture.\n\nViewed independently, these may appear to be conventional CSR projects. Together, they reflect a wider recognition that healthy businesses ultimately depend on healthy communities and healthy ecosystems.\n\nThis philosophy is aligned with the broader ESG direction of the China Merchants Port network, while reflecting Sri Lanka\u2019s local environmental and social priorities.\n\nGovernance provides the foundation for these efforts. At CICT, sustainability is supported by clear accountability, internationally certified management systems, robust safety and environmental management practices, strong compliance controls, and the integration of ESG considerations into operational and investment decisions. CICT maintains certification to internationally recognised standards, including ISO 14001 (Environmental Management Systems), ISO 45001 (Occupational Health and Safety Management Systems). These internationally recognised frameworks promote disciplined management, continuous improvement and operational excellence, ensuring that environmental and social commitments are translated into measurable, transparent and accountable action.\n\nThe maritime industry is now entering a period in which customers, investors and regulators are placing increasing emphasis on ESG performance. Cleaner supply chains, lower emissions and responsible governance are becoming competitive advantages rather than compliance requirements.\n\nThis presents both a challenge and an opportunity for ports."
