    "chunk_size_bytes": 1647,
    "chunk_text": "El Ni\u00f1o could alter weather patterns, bringing rains to some areas and dry spells to others\n\nColombo, July 24 (Daily Mirror) - El Ni\u00f1o could lead to significant changes in weather patterns, with some areas experiencing excessive rainfall while others could face dry conditions, Meteorology Department Director General Ajith Wijemannage warned.\n\nAddressing the media, Wijemannage said that during the peak phase of the El Ni\u00f1o condition, Sri Lanka could experience either higher-than-normal rainfall or drought conditions.\n\nHe said that according to the latest global forecasts, the El Ni\u00f1o phenomenon, which was previously in a weak phase, is gradually developing towards a moderate phase. The probability of it reaching its peak between October and December this year has increased to 81 percent.\n\nWijemannage cautioned that the impact of El Ni\u00f1o could result in extreme weather variations across the country, including heavy rainfall in certain areas and prolonged dry conditions in others.\n\nThe department had earlier predicted during a media briefing that the El Ni\u00f1o condition would peak between November and January next year, with a probability of 63 percent.\n\nHe said increased rainfall is expected during the last few days of this month and the first few days of next month in the Western and Sabaragamuwa provinces, Kandy and Nuwara Eliya districts, as well as Galle and Matara areas.\n\nMeanwhile, other parts of the country may experience dry weather conditions during the following month, along with an increase in the temperature felt by the human body.\n\nThe department expects considerable rainfall towards the end of September."
  },
