    "chunk_size_bytes": 1421,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka seeks China aid for bamboo, forest management\n\nColombo, July 24 (Daily Mirror) - Environment Deputy Minister Anton Jayakody has called for international cooperation to tackle the global environmental crisis, saying no country can face environmental challenges alone.\n\nAddressing a ministerial-level workshop on \u201cForest Resource Management and Green Development\u201d under the Global Development Initiative (GDI) in China, Jayakody said countries must work together to find solutions and promote sustainable development.\n\nThe workshop, held from July 24 to 30, brings together environmental ministers, officials and experts from Sri Lanka, Egypt, Ethiopia, The Gambia, Liberia, Malaysia, Namibia and Thailand.\n\nThanking the Chinese Government for hosting the event, the Deputy Minister highlighted Sri Lanka\u2019s commitment to sustainable environmental management and climate resilience.\n\nDuring the meeting, Jayakody requested China\u2019s support for three key areas \u2014 developing Sri Lanka\u2019s bamboo industry, managing forest fires, and improving the country\u2019s Forest College and training programmes.\n\nThe Sri Lankan delegation included Ministry of Environment officials and Director General of the Department of Forest Conservation P.S.C. Palamakumbura.\n\nThe Ministry said the discussions are expected to strengthen cooperation between Sri Lanka and China and support Sri Lanka\u2019s green development efforts."
