    "chunk_size_bytes": 1859,
    "chunk_text": "According to police, three Chinese nationals who had arrived at Colombo Port City in a rented cab were ambushed while fishing near the suspension bridge between 2:00 a.m. and 3:00 a.m. on Thursday by around 20 attackers who arrived in a black van. The assailants allegedly threatened them with pistols, tied and blindfolded them, and assaulted them before taking two victims in the van and the third in another vehicle.\n\nDuring the investigation, police recovered a Glock 44 semi-automatic pistol, a loaded magazine, and a VVK walkie-talkie from the abandoned van. Two Chinese nationals suspected of involvement in the crime were arrested near Bandaranaike International Airport (BIA) while allegedly attempting to flee the country.\n\nMeanwhile, Police Spokesman ASP F.U. Wootler said that police suspect that the incident stemmed from a financial dispute between two Chinese groups operating in Sri Lanka. However, he ruled out the involvement of any Sri Lankan nationals in the crime.\n\nHe said that two police teams have been deployed to continue investigations and arrest the remaining suspects involved in the attack.\n\nMeanwhile, following the incident, the Chinese Embassy in Colombo said it is closely monitoring the investigation and has urged the Sri Lanka Police to conduct a thorough inquiry, establish the facts as quickly as possible, and safeguard the safety and lawful rights of Chinese citizens in Sri Lanka.\n\nThe Embassy also advised Chinese nationals residing in or visiting Sri Lanka to remain vigilant, strengthen their personal safety awareness, promptly report emergencies to the police, and contact the Embassy for consular assistance when necessary.\n\nThe incident comes amid increasing concerns about foreign nationals engaging in online financial fraud and telecommunications scam operations while staying in Sri Lanka on tourist visas."
