    "md5": "fcba733b23b00c550c2ff6e7b49c5e8e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1973,
    "chunk_text": "Seasonal Uvas draw strong buyer interest as broader tea market stays uneven\n\nThe Leafy Orthodox teas continued to sell well, notwithstanding some price fluctuations week-on-week. The Orthodox/Rotovane BOP/BOPFs continued to witness a sluggish market, with many teas remaining unsold, due to a lack of suitable bids.\n\nThe Ex-Estate offerings totalled 0.89 M/Kgs. The overall quality from the Western and Nuwara Eliya planting districts showed no change, with a greater majority of the offerings comprising of fair average quality teas.\n\nA select range of the Uvas from the Malwatte Valley Region showed improvement in seasonal character, whilst the others showed no significant improvement in quality.\n\nThe teas from the Western planting districts \u2013 the Best available BOPs, were neglected and mostly unsold, with only a handful of invoices selling at firm to dearer rates, following special inquiry.\n\nThe corresponding BOPFs were firm and Rs.20-40 per kilogramme easier. In the Below Best category, the BOPs were firm and up to Rs.50 per kilogramme easier, whilst the corresponding BOPFs declined by Rs.20 per kilogramme and more following quality.\n\nAt the lower end of the market, majority of the BOPs remained unsold, due to substantially lower bids, whilst the corresponding BOPFs were irregular and at times declined by up to Rs.50 per kilogramme, where sold.\n\nThe Nuwara Eliyas continued to be a concerning feature, as a majority of the offerings remained unsold.\n\nThe Uda Pussellawas too continued to be poorly supported, with a majority of the offerings representing the teas at the lower end of the market.\n\nThe Uvas \u2013 Initial seasonal offerings, which came up for sale this week, met with excellent demand and the prices for the BOP/BOPFs ranged between Rs.2,100-3,000 per kilogramme and Rs.2,200-2,450 per kilogramme, respectively.\n\nThe CTCs in the High and Medium Grown category \u2013 the availability of the BP1s, were very limited, with hardly any offerings."
