    "chunk_size_bytes": 1838,
    "chunk_text": "Black July   survivors/victims still await justice\n\nJuly 23 marked that fateful day in 1983, when the Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam (LTTE) ambushed a Sri Lankan Army patrol in the northern city of Jaffna, killing 13 soldiers.\u00a0 Beginning July 24, government-backed mobs systematically targeted the Tamil population across the country.\n\nAn estimated 3,000 Tamils were killed. Around 8,000 homes and thousands of business places were destroyed, causing millions of dollars in damage. It took twenty-one years before President Chandrika Kumaratunge formally acknowledged the tragedy and distributed symbolic compensation to a group of victims\u00a0 July 21 2004.\n\nWith the riots being largely enabled by the ruling United National Party (UNP), not a single individual has ever been criminally prosecuted or convictedfor the crimes committed during the 1983 Black July pogrom. Despite widespread massacres, sexual violence, and arson targeting the Tamil community, a persistent culture of impunity still pervades.\n\nThe blood-letting and mayhem of those weeks after\u00a0 July 23 led to the near three-decade long civil war. During that period, the LTTE attacked and pillaged Sinhalese and Muslim villages. No precise numbers of Sinhala and Muslim civilians killed by the LTTE exist.\n\nHowever, nearly 72,000 Northern Province Muslims were forcibly expelled from their homes by the LTTE. Hundreds of Muslim civilians also died in massacres and localised raids carried out by the LTTE in the North and East (such as the Palliyagodella (October 15, 1992) and Kattankudy mosque massacres (August 3, 1990). No prosecutions.\n\nEven while the civil war was raging, in the south an armed uprising to topple the state led by the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP) broke out in 1987 against the presence of Indian troops in the Indian Peace Keeping Force (IPKF)."
