    "chunk_text": "The first session, chaired by Dr. Praveen Weeratunga, focused on History and Heritage. Dr. Buddhika Dassanayake delivered a compelling presentation examining the forgotten royal burial grounds of the Kandyan Kingdom and argued that the destruction of this sacred landscape amounted to a deliberate erasure of historical memory. He combined archaeological evidence, historical records and colonial documentation to reconstruct one of the island\u2019s least appreciated heritage sites.\n\nThe second session on Art, Architecture and Shared Cultural Influences, chaired by architect Lakmith Fernando, highlighted the relationship between the built environment, culture and sustainability. Dr. Priyantha Udagedara explored the colonial transformation of Nuwara Eliya through paintings, travel accounts and visual culture, illustrating how British landscape aesthetics reshaped both physical space and cultural perceptions of the hill country. Together, the presentations generated considerable discussion on heritage conservation, identity and the relevance of traditional knowledge in the modern world. Architect Sunela Jayewardene delivered a thought-provoking address on Sri Lanka\u2019s traditional architectural heritage, demonstrating how centuries-old indigenous design principles provide practical solutions to contemporary environmental challenges, climate resilience and sustainable living."
