    "chunk_size_bytes": 1641,
    "chunk_text": "Encouraged by the response to the novel, two years later, in 1916, he began writing to the press. His first article, \u201cVruksalatha saha Sathwayo\u201d (\u201cPlants and Animals\u201d), published in a leading daily Sri Lankan newspaper, the Dinamina, posed a daring question: if higher forms of life such as animals and humans were subject to the laws of reincarnation as dictated by Buddhism, shouldn\u2019t lower forms of life, such as plants, be subject to them as well? Going beyond even his critique of tradition in Leela, this first article opened him to a barrage of angry comments by readers. Once again, he had resolved to go against the grain and to use his knowledge to reinterpret his faith and culture.\n\nFirst, in Sinhala, Wickramasinghe introduced literary, cultural, and scientific terms and ideas to a vernacular audience. At a time when Sinhala nationalists uncritically valorized the past and held it as an object of veneration, Wickramasinghe popularized the most contemporary, radical ideas from the West among his countrymen. In doing so he made them aware of other ways of framing identity, beyond the restrictive, often insular forms which nationalism took, and has taken since then, in Sri Lanka.\n\nSecond, writing in English, he applied the frameworks of the leading anthropologists of his time \u2013 the two most prominent being Bronislaw Malinowski and Ruth Benedict \u2013 to his community. As the anthropologist Sarath Amunugama noted in an interview with me, in delving into his society so deeply, he made Sinhala Buddhist culture \u201cas valid an object of study as any other rural traditional society in the world of his time.\u201d"
