    "chunk_size_bytes": 2024,
    "chunk_text": "Compliance clock ticks for EU-bound exporters\n\nSri Lankan exporters targeting the European Union (EU) have been urged to begin preparing immediately for a series of stringent sustainability regulations that will take effect from next year, with non-compliance carrying the risk of substantial financial penalties and possible loss of market access.\n\nAccording to Peterson Solutions Sri Lanka, the new regulatory framework will significantly change how companies market products, manage supply chains and substantiate environmental and social responsibility claims.\n\nThe first major requirement will come into force on September 27, 2026, under the EU\u2019s Empowering Consumers for the Green Transition Directive (EmpCo), aimed at eliminating misleading environmental claims, commonly referred to as \u201cgreenwashing\u201d.\n\nAsia Pacific Regional Manager of Peterson Solutions Sri Lanka, Dr. Rukshan Gunatilaka, said businesses would no longer be able to make broad claims such as \u201cgreen\u201d, \u201ceco-friendly\u201d or \u201csustainably produced\u201d without credible evidence to support them.\n\n\u201cThe era of simply saying a product is \u2018green\u2019, \u2018eco-friendly\u2019 or \u2018sustainably produced\u2019 is coming to an end. Businesses must now be able to produce credible evidence supporting every sustainability claim they make,\u201d he said.\n\nHe warned that companies found making unsubstantiated sustainability claims could face penalties of up to \u20ac2 million or 4% of annual turnover in the relevant EU member state, in addition to reputational damage and restrictions on market access.\n\nDr. Gunatilaka noted that the rules extend beyond product labels and apply to websites, advertisements, social media content, sustainability reports and other promotional material.\n\nHe said exporters must also prepare for the EU Packaging and Packaging Waste Regulation (PPWR), which begins applying from August 12, 2026, and the EU Deforestation Regulation (EUDR), whose due diligence obligations take effect for larger companies on December 30, 2026."
  },
