    "md5": "7102395748edebde2838fa7e371485f3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1711,
    "chunk_text": "Compact crossovers lead auto recovery as Chinese brands gain ground\n\nThe SUV and crossover category emerged as the strongest-performing passenger vehicle segment in June 2026, driving the broader market recovery as consumer preferences shift toward practical urban crossovers and Chinese automakers capture market share.\n\nAccording to the latest vehicle registration commentary by JB Securities CEO/MD Murtaza Jafferjee, total SUV and crossover registrations rose to 5,933 units in June from 5,511 units in May, propelled entirely by a surge in brand-new unit registrations.\n\nCountering the widespread perception that the growth of SUVs increases overall fuel consumption across the country, detailed technical classifications reveal that small engine capacities dominate the market segment. Crossovers with engine displacement below 1,500cc accounted for an overwhelming 96 percent of all SUV and crossover registrations during the month.\n\n\u201cCrossovers below 1,500cc continued to dominate the SUV market accounting for 96 percent of total registrations, far from the popular perception that SUVs are fuel guzzling vehicles which is clearly not the case in Sri Lanka. Most of these vehicles are really crossovers - more like hatchbacks rather than full size SUVs,\u201d Jafferjee stated in his analysis.\n\nToyota maintained its overall segment leadership with 1,965 registrations in June, led heavily by compact utility offerings such as the Raize with 1,448 units, the Urban Cruiser with 309 units, and the Yaris Cross with 97 units.\n\nHonda followed with 1,209 registrations, almost exclusively represented by the Vezel with 1,175 units, while Suzuki recorded 599 units, driven almost entirely by its Fronx model."
