    "chunk_text": "Toyota maintained its overall segment leadership with 1,965 registrations in June, led heavily by compact utility offerings such as the Raize with 1,448 units, the Urban Cruiser with 309 units, and the Yaris Cross with 97 units.\n\nHonda followed with 1,209 registrations, almost exclusively represented by the Vezel with 1,175 units, while Suzuki recorded 599 units, driven almost entirely by its Fronx model.\n\nJafferjee highlighted that Chinese marques are gaining significant traction among Sri Lankan buyers through competitive technology and price positioning. Beyond BYD\u2014which saw strong demand for its Sealion SUV with 351 units and Atto variants with 165 units\u2014several other Chinese manufacturers maintained a meaningful market presence, including Jetour with 148 units, BAIC with 110 units, Jaecoo with 96 units, GAC with 91 units, Changan with 36 units, and SAIC with 31 units.\n\nThe surge in Chinese imports is equally prominent in the two-wheeler sector, which remains the country\u2019s largest vehicle category with 40,007 total registrations in June. Electric two-wheeler registrations witnessed a sharp rebound, jumping to 6,426 units from 4,625 units in May. This expansion was driven almost entirely by Chinese electric motorbike brands, with Yadea leading the segment with 3,287 units, followed by Aima with 592 units."
  },
