    "description": "Credit surge masked as auto dealers dodge inventory penalty",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business-main/Credit-surge-masked-as-auto-dealers-dodge-inventory-penalty/245-346687",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785095138.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-27-dailymirrorlk-2ba11789-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "031ae4e8acecc164266fc7e9c286071f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1805,
    "chunk_text": "Credit surge masked as auto dealers dodge inventory penalty\n\nDealers preempt 3% monthly inventory penalty by pre-registering vehicles, artificially depressing official registration-based financing statistics\n\nNon-bank financial institutions rapidly expand their balance sheets through commercial bank borrowings to fund consumer and vehicle loans\n\nDespite a 27.8% surge in private sector credit, strong government fiscal discipline anchors overall domestic credit growth\n\nOfficial vehicle registration statistics are presenting a deceptive picture of auto-financing activity in Sri Lanka. Motor dealers are increasingly pre-registering unsold vehicles under their own names to avoid inventory penalties, while non-bank financial institutions aggressively expand their balance sheets to fund vehicle purchases, according to an analysis by JB Securities CEO/MD Murtaza Jafferjee.\n\nDepartment of Motor Traffic data shows financing penetration hovering at a subdued 43 percent across passenger vehicles in June 2026. However, underlying financial system data reveals a sharp contrast. Private sector credit expanded by a robust 27.8 percent year-on-year in May 2026, driven largely by consumer finance, pawning, and non-bank financial institution lending.\n\nThis divergence stems from tactical moves by vehicle importers to bypass fiscal regulations. Authorities currently levy a monthly penalty equivalent to the percent of a vehicle\u2019s CIF (Cost, Insurance and Freight) value if it remains unregistered beyond 90 days after importation. Introduced to prevent overstocking and current account pressures, the policy has instead led dealers to register vehicles prior to retail sale. This effectively clears the statutory window while masking true leasing and mortgage volumes in official registration records."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
