    "md5": "c68554c32acaa769f1952af03a61743f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1487,
    "chunk_text": "Integrity Begins Within:  Internal Affairs Units,  the Keystone of a  New Public Service Culture\n\nThe state is finally responding to the people\u2019s long-felt need to end corruption\n\nSri Lanka has now laid many of the legal and institutional foundations necessary for meaningful reform. Nevertheless, legislation alone cannot transform governance\n\nAs Sri Lanka marks Integrity Week from 27 July, the occasion offers more than an opportunity for symbolic reflection. It provides a timely moment to assess whether the country\u2019s recent anti-corruption reforms are beginning to reshape public services in a meaningful and sustainable manner. Following years of economic turmoil, declining public confidence, and increasing demands for accountability, Sri Lanka has embarked on its most ambitious anti-corruption reform agenda in decades.\n\nYet the ultimate success of these reforms will not be determined solely by new laws, investigations or prosecutions. It will depend on whether integrity becomes embedded within the everyday operations of public institutions. In this context, the establishment of Internal Affairs Units (IAUs) across public sector organisations may prove to be one of the most transformative reforms introduced under the new anti-corruption framework.\n\nIf properly empowered, adequately resourced and protected from political interference, these units have the potential to shift Sri Lanka\u2019s anti corruption efforts from reactive enforcement to proactive prevention."
  },
