    "md5": "ee201b5b353c026de40d98aa2b4e82dd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1951,
    "chunk_text": "These countries increasingly view corruption prevention as a core responsibility of every public institution rather than the exclusive mandate of specialised enforcement agencies. The common lesson is clear. Countries achieve lasting improvements not because they prosecute more offenders, but because they build institutions where corruption becomes increasingly difficult to commit and easier to detect.\n\nMost importantly, integrity should become a shared responsibility rather than the exclusive function of specialised units. Every public servant, regardless of seniority, contributes either to strengthening or weakening institutional integrity through everyday decisions. Integrity as a National Value Integrity Week should therefore be understood not simply as a commemorative event but as a reminder that national development depends fundamentally upon public trust. Economic growth, foreign investment, social justice and democratic governance all require institutions that citizens believe are fair, accountable and transparent. Sri Lanka\u2019s recent anti-corruption reforms have created an encouraging framework for change.\n\nThe establishment of Internal Affairs Units signals a welcome recognition that preventing corruption is far more effective than merely responding to it after damage has occurred. Their long-term success, however, will depend not only on legal mandates but also on leadership, professionalism, institutional independence and an unwavering commitment to ethical public service. If these conditions are sustained, Integrity Week may come to represent not merely an annual observance, but the beginning of a lasting transformation in the culture of governance\u2014one in which integrity is no longer regarded as an aspiration, but as the defining characteristic of Sri Lankan public institutions.\n\n(Tharindu Dananjaya Weerasinghe is a Senior Lecturer of the Department of Human Resource Management, University of Kelaniya)"
