    "chunk_size_bytes": 1904,
    "chunk_text": "Fresh controversy over Colombo national school's dry ration drive\n\nColombo, July 27 (Daily Mirror) - Fresh controversy has erupted over the disputed dry ration collection drive at Thurstan College after the school's parents' union alleged that some of the donated food items had already expired and claimed the distribution programme had been shifted from Delft Island (Neduntheevu) to schools in Jaffna following media scrutiny.\n\nThe latest allegations come weeks after the parents' union claimed that Thurstan College had requested parents of students from Grades 1 to 13 to donate dry rations and school supplies for around 600 students in 10 schools on Delft Island, raising concerns over compulsory collections and the financial burden placed on families.\n\nAccording to the parents' union, the principal had informed parents that the initiative was being conducted as part of a Health Promotion Programme under the Education Ministry. However, the union said Education Ministry officials later confirmed they were unaware of any such programme and that no approval had been granted.\n\nThe union also alleged that parents had been instructed not to publicise the collection drive.\n\nIn the latest development, the parents' union claimed that some of the dry rations collected from parents had already expired despite plans to distribute them on Tuesday (29). It further alleged that, following media scrutiny of the controversy, the principal abandoned the original plan to distribute the goods to schools on Delft Island and instead decided to send them to several schools in Jaffna.\n\nAccording to the union, the goods are now expected to be handed over to the Jaffna Zonal Education Office.\n\nThe union also claimed that teachers had been instructed to report for duty and travel to Jaffna to participate in the distribution programme despite the project allegedly lacking Education Ministry approval."
