    "language": "en",
    "md5": "408945e27d54b8d0e530dc1eb9a54212",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1596,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cMost of these Russian and Ukrainian visitors trade in crypto to get funds in and out of the country and to pay for things locally,\u201d Perera stated.\n\nHe suggested that this shift to the crypto space should be closely evaluated alongside the depreciation of the rupee and changing tourist demographics.\n\nHe noted that the current regulations impose a 20 percent migration tax and limit the initial migration allowances to US $ 100,000, which has inadvertently driven the individuals toward the grey-market methods to bypass the formal banking sector, despite the associated legal risks.\n\nTo address these systemic leaks, Perera stressed the necessity of a more proactive and holistic regulatory approach.\n\n\u201cWe need to move towards a more comprehensive framework that also recognises these as an asset class and has provisions for licensing, taxation and consumer protection,\u201d he urged, noting that Sri Lanka could take guidance from jurisdictions such as the United Kingdom, Australia and Singapore.\n\nIn a promising shift from previous Central Bank warnings, the government has recently accelerated efforts to formalise the sector. In June 2026, the Securities and Exchange Commission and Digital Economy Ministry launched a joint initiative to explore and regulate virtual assets, aiming to develop a comprehensive regulatory roadmap.\n\nFurthermore, the government\u2019s Sub Committee on Virtual Asset Service Providers, chaired by Digital Economy Deputy Minister Eranga Weeraratne, has been actively advancing a regulatory framework to strengthen the oversight in the rapidly evolving space."
  },
