    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "641acd09c6eea6ee26983acea54c863c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1865,
    "chunk_text": "Unresolved VAT disputes could prolong prosecution risk for businesses: EY\n\nThe Sri Lankan businesses facing unresolved Value Added Tax (VAT) disputes could remain exposed to prosecution for an extended period under the new amendments that significantly widen the country\u2019s tax enforcement framework, according to professional services firm EY.\n\nThe Value Added Tax (Amendment) Act, No. 14 of 2026, allows the criminal proceedings for the VAT offences to be instituted within 12 years of the date an offence was committed. Where the offence involves a failure to perform a statutory obligation, the period begins from the date on which that obligation was due.\n\nHowever, the exposure could extend further in cases involving non-disclosure or misrepresentation affecting a taxpayer\u2019s VAT liability. In such instances, the prosecution period begins only from the date on which the correct liability is finally determined.\n\nEY said this could prolong the period of exposure where the audits, disputes, reviews or appeals remain unresolved, raising the stakes for the businesses with historical VAT matters.\n\nThe provision could require the companies to pay closer attention to legacy transactions, supporting documents and record retention, particularly where the VAT assessments remain under challenge.\n\nThe amendments also authorise the Attorney-General or an authorised person to prosecute the VAT offences, confirming that such violations may proceed through a formal criminal process where warranted.\n\nThe maximum monetary penalty for a range of VAT offences has meanwhile increased fortyfold. The offences committed before October 1, 2025 carry a maximum fine of Rs.25,000, imprisonment of up to six months or both. For the offences committed on or after that date, the maximum fine rises to Rs.1 million, while the possible prison term remains at six months."
  },
