    "md5": "e7b3cecedabc3a4125cacc3a1e9b0781",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1965,
    "chunk_text": "The maximum monetary penalty for a range of VAT offences has meanwhile increased fortyfold. The offences committed before October 1, 2025 carry a maximum fine of Rs.25,000, imprisonment of up to six months or both. For the offences committed on or after that date, the maximum fine rises to Rs.1 million, while the possible prison term remains at six months.\n\nFailure to provide valid tax invoices, customs goods declarations or other required documents may similarly lead to a fine of up to Rs.1 million, imprisonment of up to six months or both.\n\nThe amended law also makes it an offence to obtain or attempt to obtain a VAT refund through fraud, misrepresentation, false or misleading information, fraudulent documents or the concealment of material facts.\n\nThe separate amendments introduced through the Inland Revenue (Amendment) Act, No. 11 of 2026, expand enforcement beyond administrative penalties by allowing prosecution for several forms of tax non-compliance.\n\nThese include failures to register with the Commissioner General of Inland Revenue, file income tax returns, furnish specified tax returns, submit annual statements or appear before the Commissioner General following a statutory notice.\n\nProsecution will not begin automatically. The Inland Revenue Department must first issue a written notice and give the taxpayer an opportunity to rectify the default. A conviction may result in a fine of up to Rs.400,000, imprisonment of up to six months or both.\n\nThe amendments also allow the department to recover the unpaid taxes through the Magistrate\u2019s Court proceedings, where the amount in default may be treated as a court-imposed fine. This mechanism will not apply while a review or appeal remains pending.\n\nEY cautioned that the Inland Revenue Department\u2019s enhanced enforcement powers should be matched by greater responsibility, particularly because of the evidentiary weight attached to a certificate issued by the Commissioner General."
