    "chunk_size_bytes": 1977,
    "chunk_text": "Report of COPA inquiry conducted on October 24 last year on the scheme implemented from 1 May 2022 to 15 September 2023 for issuing permits to Sri Lankans employed overseas to import fully electric vehicles using foreign remittances is thus to be referred to the Attorney General.\n\nWe salute the Parliament for amending the Standing Orders that enabled the House to allow its key oversight committees to refer their findings on shady deals to the AG and the CIABOC and for adopting these subsequent two motions under those amendments.\n\nThe Daily Mirror has been pressing the authorities over the years to find a mechanism to take follow up action on corruption in public institutions that have been revealed during the deliberations of the COPE, COPA and Committee on Public Finance (COPF), pointing out that the sessions of these committees have become mere media shows due to absence of such follow up actions.\n\nSeveral Chairmen and members of these committees had also made suggestions during the past few years to develop mechanisms to refer the negative findings of these committees that had sometimes exposed corruption involving billions of rupees, to the relevant law enforcement authorities.\u00a0 Samagi Jana Balawegaya (SJB) MP Eran Wickramaratne on October 25, 2021, questioned in Parliament the purpose of the COPE if it cannot take punitive action against frauds and corruption in State-owned entities that were being exposed during the deliberations of that committee.\n\nThen, Prof. Charitha Herath as the Chairman of the COPE in 2022 suggested during proceedings of his committee to take up the follow-up issue in Parliament and create a mechanism for action against the culprits. In October 2023, COPF Chairman Dr. Harsha De Silva told Parliament that having a representative from the Attorney General\u2019s Department present during meetings of the PAC and COPE would facilitate the utilisation of evidence uncovered during such meetings in future court proceedings."
  },
