    "md5": "1554cd3aa2006e91b9368cb7ff3192f1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1978,
    "chunk_text": "Compared to this, 56 people have died of dengue so far in 2026.\u00a0 Wild elephants killed over 100 people in 2025, while between 100-400 people die of snakebite any given year. What about deaths caused by alcohol-related driving? Overall traffic accidents claim an average of about 2,500 to 3,000 lives each year, with alcohol consumption identified as a contributing factor in roughly 4.5% to over 5% of investigated road traffic accidents. During this year\u2019s Vesak festival, a drunken double\u00a0 cab\u00a0 driver\u00a0 killed\u00a0 eight people waiting at a dan sal queue. Even if we take the lower figure of 2,500, we have 25 deaths per year due to alcohol-related driving accidents. But there is no debate or any no serious protest regarding the cause \u2013 alcohol consumption.\n\nBy comparison, Sri Lanka spends an estimated national average of $242 per\u00a0 case on direct treatment and hospitalisation for dengue, while community prevention via vector control programs costs around $400,496 annually ($2 per person protected). If the country implements a national dengue vaccination strategy, that cost is projected to rise to $28.95 million annually.\n\nThe government health sector is cash-strapped and short of essential medicines. Many politicians, bureaucrats and doctors see rabies prevention as a waste of precious money. The problem here is really one of attitude. We stopped killing stray dogs using unspeakable methods only two decades ago. There are many in positions of power who see it as the easy way out of a thorny problem. But, since the culling of street dogs is now illegal, the easier course would be to starve them to death by intimidating the feeders.\n\nBut those who think we have a serious rabies problem should look at India. India has an estimated 60-70 million street dogs, and up to 20,000 people die of rabies each year. But, like in Sri Lanka, many people are involved in feeding and caring for street dogs, though the task is\u00a0 overwhelming compared to resources."
  },
