    "md5": "84c2ccb92010f16203731b5d2fbc3a9c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1993,
    "chunk_text": "But those who think we have a serious rabies problem should look at India. India has an estimated 60-70 million street dogs, and up to 20,000 people die of rabies each year. But, like in Sri Lanka, many people are involved in feeding and caring for street dogs, though the task is\u00a0 overwhelming compared to resources.\n\nThis was both humane and logical. We should follow this example. A recent article in this paper by a group of animal welfare organisations highlighted the urgent need for a national animal action plan focusing on sterilisation and vaccination which should enlist the support of community feeders and welfare groups. This is common sense and a practical idea.\n\nBut they asked the question: \u201cWe\u00a0 are ready to work with the government: But is the government ready to work with us?\u201d This is an important question. This writer has been a community feeder and care giver for street dogs and cats for three decades, and knows from first hand experience how lethargic municipality and other state dog-handling, anti-rabies bodies are.\u00a0 Even in better times, they lacked motivation, from vets down to dog catchers. What is primarily needed is motivation.\n\nEach municipality has a dog catcher team. Over the years, I have seen them descending into grumbling and lethargy, complaining about the heat, and how hard it is to catch street dogs. They complain about lacking nets. The government should look into such needs. Once, I met the municipal vet in my area going around\u00a0 in an air-conditioned double cab, taking a census of stray dogs. They do have resources.\u00a0 But, After Covid, they began asking people to bring stray dogs in for vaccination and sterilisation, which is very difficult for individual feeders and caregivers.\n\nOfficials need to be motivated. Without that, the street dog issue cannot be solved no matter how much funding is available. and the government should have the\u00a0 good sense to enlist the help of animal welfare organisations and community feeders."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
