    "chunk_size_bytes": 2013,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe reasons which have been put forward by the government for the constitutional amendment pertaining to the retirement age of judges are that it is done to do away with the backlog of cases. There is no point in extending the retirement age of Supreme Court and Appeal Court judges as most of the backlog cases are pending in magistrate and district courts. Also, there are a number of laws which can be amended to ease out the backlog. One is the amendment of divorce laws to ease the pending divorce cases,\u201d he said.\n\nPresident\u2019s Counsel Srinath Perera said all people who love the nation and democracy should come out against this move. \u201cIf there are any person in Sri Lanka who loves the nation and are concerned on his or her rights should come forward against this move,\u201d he said.\n\nPresident's Counsel and Acting General Secretary of ITAK M. A. Sumanthiran said it is the Executive Presidency which could be abolished if the government wants to amend the constitution in a hurry. \u201c It was the incumbent Minister Vijitha Herath who presented a Private Members Bill to abolish the Executive Presidency when he was in the opposition. \u201c I request the opposition MPs to re-present this Bill to Parliament and see how the government would react to it,\u201d he said.\n\nPresident\u2019s Counsel Geoffrey Alagaratnam said none of the members in the legal fraternity who oppose this move by the government is prepared to play politics but only want to safeguard democracy. \u201cThis Constitution belongs to the people. It does not belong to Parliament or the President. It is a democratic constitution. Democracy means people. Once we elect a President and Parliament is elected, they should consult the people and do what they want. The judiciary is the last place people could go to seek justice; therefore, its independence is important. This amendment is to capture the court. I don\u2019t see much difference between a bribe and this constitutional amendment as it gives something to the judges,\u201d he said."
