    "chunk_size_bytes": 1642,
    "chunk_text": "\"The Treasury Secretary is responsible for public funds, while the Finance Minister must take responsibility on behalf of the political leadership of the government.\" The report clearly states that there were shortcomings in the internal handling of the transition, the provision of guidelines and the administration. However, no allegations have been made against any senior official. Instead, only lower-ranking officers have been blamed. Accordingly, four officials have already been interdicted for neglect of duty. The report refers only to issues relating to language and coordination. This amounts to double standards. Higher officials who designed the systems appear to have defended themselves. Negligence relating to IT systems actually invited cyber thieves to act.\n\n\"The government has attempted to portray the theft as a sophisticated cybercrime. This is not the truth.\n\n\"There was an 18-month period to transfer debt management powers from the Central Bank to the Public Debt Management Office. The Public Debt Management Office was established in December 2024. A coordination committee was required to be set up for the transition under Section 8 of the Public Debt Management Act. This committee was responsible for implementing work assigned by the Finance Minister. However, the minister failed to appoint the relevant persons in terms of Section 7 of the Act. The Central Bank signed a memorandum of understanding (MoU) with the Public Debt Management Office in January 2026, while the theft had already taken place between November 2025 and January 2026. Therefore, the government has concealed the truth from the public."
