    "chunk_text": "Both French fire zones faced a fresh test as temperatures started rising again on Tuesday. France\u2019s national weather service placed Gironde under a yellow heat warning from midday, forecasting inland temperatures of 33 to 35C. South-east winds were expected across the department, along with westerly sea breezes on the coast. According to the Interior Ministry, more than 116,000 hectares have burned across France so far this year. The European Union\u2019s Copernicus climate service said western Europe recorded its hottest June on record, with extreme heat and widespread dryness helping fires spread and intensify in France and Spain.\n\nAuthorities in Spain have evacuated about 79,000 people and ordered another 30,000 to remain in their homes during a crisis that led the government to declare a national emergency for wildfires for the first time. The biggest concern remained two large fires in wooded hills about an hour\u2019s drive west of Madrid that had at times nearly merged into a single mega-blaze. Another fire in the eastern province of Castell\u0192\u00f3n also forced mass evacuations.\n\nA fire burning through hilly rural terrain in vila, west of Madrid, has become Spain\u2019s largest on record, scorching more than 500 square kilometres, the government said. That is five times the size of Barcelona. \u201cLook at how the valley is burned. Look at the houses, the people,\u201d said David Gonzalez, a 48-year-old gardener who used his van to deliver supplies to firefighters in vila. \u201cWe are feeling a lot of anger and helplessness. It\u2019s something inhuman,\u201d he said.\n\nSpain\u2019s Ecology Minister Sara Aagesen said fires have burned 1,530 square kilometres in the country this year, six times the area scorched by blazes in the first half of 2025. Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez has called for a national pact to fight wildfires worsened by climate change. Across France and Spain, authorities were trying to hold the line as heat, wind and drought continued to threaten fresh flare-ups."
