    "chunk_size_bytes": 1290,
    "chunk_text": "S&P highlighted that government revenue increased significantly, supported by tax reforms and economic recovery. Revenue rose by 34% in 2025 to 16.7% of GDP, while the fiscal deficit narrowed to 2.3% of GDP.\n\nRevenue growth continued in the first five months of 2026, increasing by 30% compared to the same period last year, the agency said.\n\nThe ratings agency said the Middle East conflict could affect Sri Lanka through higher energy prices, reduced tourism earnings and slower remittance growth, as the region accounts for a significant share of foreign worker remittances.\n\nIt also warned that a stronger El Ni\u00f1o event in 2026 and 2027 could pose risks to agriculture, electricity generation and overall economic activity.\n\nS&P said Sri Lanka\u2019s continued commitment to reforms under the International Monetary Fund (IMF) programme, including improving government revenue, cost recovery in utilities and strengthening public financial management, would remain important for further economic improvement.\n\nThe agency said it could upgrade Sri Lanka\u2019s ratings if stronger economic growth leads to further improvements in fiscal and external positions. However, the ratings could be downgraded if renewed funding pressures or a significant deterioration in economic conditions emerge."
