    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "aedf2e0cbb5670e934d1f732387f9cce",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 906,
    "chunk_text": "Manipulation by self-appointed brokers\n\nWe would like to get involved in this matter, address more issues that house hunters face.\n\nAs we agree on the\u00a0 argument is that \u201ctenant protection cannot exist without corresponding tenant responsibility\u201d there other issues that needs to be addressed.\n\n1. Self appointed brokers and realtors manipulate by representing the landlords only\u00a0 and convincing them to charge exorbitant rents for broker commissions.\n\n2. The Brokers do represent or negotiate on behalf of the house hunters, yet they demand for half a month\u2019s rent as their fees, if we disagree to pay, the don\u2019t allow us to speak to the owners or inspect the house.\n\n3. The landlords charge exorbitant rents, almost the cost of mortgage prices from a bank.\n\n4. Some houses are categorised as luxury and they charge between Rs. 200,000 to 300,000 because it has an extra bathroom or a third room."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-29-dailymirrorlk-9971f999",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-9971f999",
    "date_str": "2026-07-29",
    "description": "Are we governing for the next election,  or the next generation?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Are-we-governing-for-the-next-election-or-the-next-generation/172-346862",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785270752.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-29-dailymirrorlk-9971f999-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "78ec096ae14005c4aac459cb9ca1caf1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1812,
    "chunk_text": "Are we governing for the next election,  or the next generation?\n\nEvery government begins its term with a familiar list of urgent priorities. The economy needs attention. Public services demand improvement. The cost of living weighs heavily on households, while businesses seek stability and confidence to invest. These are real and immediate concerns, and no responsible government can afford to ignore them. But there is another question that deserves equal attention, even if it rarely dominates political debate. Are the decisions being made today designed simply to win the next election, or are they laying the foundations for the next generation?\n\nSri Lanka has emerged from one of the most difficult periods in its post-independence history. Inflation has eased, foreign reserves have improved and the economy has regained a measure of stability that once seemed out of reach. Those achievements should not be underestimated. They reflect painful adjustments and sacrifices made by citizens across the country. Yet stability, important as it is, should never become the final goal.\n\nThe next stage of the country\u2019s journey demands a different kind of leadership. Recovering from a crisis requires decisive action. Building a prosperous nation requires the willingness to think beyond the next five years.\n\nBut too often, national priorities have shifted with changes in political leadership. Long-term reforms have been abandoned before they had the chance to produce results. Whether it is education, transport, agriculture or economic policy, the country has repeatedly struggled to maintain continuity. This cycle comes at a cost. Businesses hesitate when policies change without warning. Investors prefer countries where long-term plans survive political transitions. Citizens too, lose confidence."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-29-dailymirrorlk-9971f999",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-9971f999",
    "date_str": "2026-07-29",
    "description": "Are we governing for the next election,  or the next generation?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Are-we-governing-for-the-next-election-or-the-next-generation/172-346862",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785270752.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-29-dailymirrorlk-9971f999-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "72f9622eec1f48c1f23b223101128d9d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1913,
    "chunk_text": "But too often, national priorities have shifted with changes in political leadership. Long-term reforms have been abandoned before they had the chance to produce results. Whether it is education, transport, agriculture or economic policy, the country has repeatedly struggled to maintain continuity. This cycle comes at a cost. Businesses hesitate when policies change without warning. Investors prefer countries where long-term plans survive political transitions. Citizens too, lose confidence.\n\nThe labour market tells a similar story. Employers in several industries report growing difficulty in finding skilled workers, even as many young Sri Lankans continue to leave the country in search of better opportunities. When skilled workers, entrepreneurs and professionals increasingly believe their future lies elsewhere, it raises uncomfortable questions about the country\u2019s own ability to create opportunity at home.\n\nThe demographic picture makes the challenge even more pressing. Sri Lanka\u2019s population is ageing, birth rates are declining and the workforce will gradually become smaller. These changes may unfold quietly, but their impact will be profound. Healthcare, pensions, productivity and economic growth will all be affected. Preparing for that future requires policies that look decades ahead.\n\nThe same principle applies to infrastructure. Development is not measured only by new highways or impressive buildings. Reliable public transport, efficient digital services, modern hospitals, dependable energy supplies and strong water management systems are equally important. These investments rarely produce immediate political dividends, but they shape the quality of life and economic competitiveness of a country for generations.\n\nClimate resilience deserves the same long-term thinking. Responding after disasters occur is no longer enough. Planning ahead is not optional; it is essential."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-29-dailymirrorlk-9971f999",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-9971f999",
    "date_str": "2026-07-29",
    "description": "Are we governing for the next election,  or the next generation?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Are-we-governing-for-the-next-election-or-the-next-generation/172-346862",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785270752.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-29-dailymirrorlk-9971f999-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fdc7069a1b188cd39ef7afd9b52cd7a7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1198,
    "chunk_text": "The same principle applies to infrastructure. Development is not measured only by new highways or impressive buildings. Reliable public transport, efficient digital services, modern hospitals, dependable energy supplies and strong water management systems are equally important. These investments rarely produce immediate political dividends, but they shape the quality of life and economic competitiveness of a country for generations.\n\nClimate resilience deserves the same long-term thinking. Responding after disasters occur is no longer enough. Planning ahead is not optional; it is essential.\n\nSri Lanka has already shown remarkable resilience. The question now is whether the country will settle for recovery or aim for something more lasting.\n\nHistory rarely remembers governments for short-term political victories. It remembers those that strengthened institutions, invested in people and made choices that continued to benefit the country long after they had left office.\n\nThat is the challenge before Sri Lanka today. The decisions made now will shape the opportunities available not only to today\u2019s citizens but also to the children who will inherit this country in the decades ahead."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-29-dailymirrorlk-b6a46dd0",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-b6a46dd0",
    "date_str": "2026-07-29",
    "description": "Prison overcrowding:  Revision of remand prisoners key to solution",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Prison-overcrowding-Revision-of-remand-prisoners-key-to-solution/172-346863",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1785271057.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-29-dailymirrorlk-b6a46dd0-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d128b90a342479b6ff874c927a024a0b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1597,
    "chunk_text": "Prison overcrowding:  Revision of remand prisoners key to solution\n\nThe most practical is to first focus on remand prisoners who amount to around 70 percent of the total\n\nThe Department reported to Parliament that there was a backlog of 17,671 reports due from the Narcotics Division by\u00a0end 2024\n\n\u201cThe degree of civilisation of a society can be judged by entering its prisons.\u201d\n\nThis aphorism is sometimes attributed to the Russian author Fyodor Dostoevsky and sometimes to the French philosopher Voltaire. Whoever said it, it rings true.\n\nDescribing the horrific overcrowding in our prisons is no longer necessary. The Negombo calamity has done that better than anyone could. What is now needed is quick action to prevent a recurrence and thereby drag our civilisation out from the ditch.\n\nThe most practical is to first focus on remand prisoners who amount to around 70 percent of the total. Just in Colombo, New Magazine and Colombo Remand Prisons held 29,745 unconvicted men in 2024, more than double the 12,592 housed in 2020. Women remandees were housed in Welikada: 2,692 in 2024 (more than double the number remanded in 2020). All these numbers are likely to be higher in 2026 because of the police wanting to score points under the President\u2019s anti-drug campaign.\n\nRemand prisoners are presumed innocent. There can be no debate about the need to treat remand prisoners in a humane manner. If effective measures can be taken to reduce the overcrowding in remand prisons, it will be easier to address problems affecting the minority who have been found guilty.\u00a0 \u00a0Sections 54 and 83"
  },
