    "md5": "35b574bf1ec662d72e4ede015e9a1506",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1950,
    "chunk_text": "Remand prisoners are presumed innocent. There can be no debate about the need to treat remand prisoners in a humane manner. If effective measures can be taken to reduce the overcrowding in remand prisons, it will be easier to address problems affecting the minority who have been found guilty.\u00a0 \u00a0Sections 54 and 83\n\nIf the government acts quickly to repeal section 83, the inflow into the remand prisons can be stanched. Is this a complete solution? No. The Magistrate\u2019s Courts should be better resourced, so that the judges can actually apply their minds to the question of whether a suspect should be given bail or not. It is not that all persons charged under section 54 will automatically be granted bail. If the police make a persuasive argument that a particular suspect must be remanded,\u00a0 the Magistrate can do so. The police will no longer be able to overuse section 54 to take people they consider as bad guys \u201coff the streets.\u201d\u00a0 Delays in Government Analyst\u2019s reports\n\nProving a case filed under the Poisons, Opium and Dangerous Drugs Ordinance requires the Magistrate to see the scientific evidence on whether the substance that is the basis for the arrest is actually a prohibited substance, and even whether it meets the thresholds set under various sections. Until the report from the Government Analyst\u2019s Department (GAD) is received, the suspects are kept in remand. T\n\nThe Department reported to Parliament that there was a backlog of 17,671 reports due from the Narcotics Division by end 2024. In 2020, the backlog was 5,873. The Department is generating more reports (27,127 in 2024 versus 16,928 in 2020), but the backlog is still increasing. The base problem is the rapidly increasing number of requests that the Department cannot keep up with.\n\nSuspects must get justice. Therefore, reducing the requests for reports is not the answer (decriminalising minor drug possessions is, but that is politically difficult)."
  },
