    "language": "en",
    "md5": "ea710cbca1ece620c9d750c34a45955e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1647,
    "chunk_text": "The Department reported to Parliament that there was a backlog of 17,671 reports due from the Narcotics Division by end 2024. In 2020, the backlog was 5,873. The Department is generating more reports (27,127 in 2024 versus 16,928 in 2020), but the backlog is still increasing. The base problem is the rapidly increasing number of requests that the Department cannot keep up with.\n\nSuspects must get justice. Therefore, reducing the requests for reports is not the answer (decriminalising minor drug possessions is, but that is politically difficult).\n\nA comprehensive assessment of GAD followed by rapid implementation actions including amendments to the relevant legislation can cut the delays and thereby reduce the overcrowding of remand prisons.\u00a0 House arrest and electronic anklets\n\nMany countries, including Australia, Brazil, New Zealand, Thailand, and Viet Nam, are keeping unconvicted prisoners out of expensive remand custody using house arrest, complemented by electronic monitoring. I proposed this solution in August 2025 and was happy to see the Minister of Justice taking it up in February 2026.\n\nIn 2019, 60,347 devices were active in Brazil, which amounted to under 9 percent of the prison population in that country. Jair Bolsanaro, the former President of Brazil was restricted to his home wearing an electronic anklet while awaiting trial. According to The National Secretariat of Penal Policies, the cost of an electronic bracelet in Brazil was around R$200 (around Rs 10,800) per remandee. It is stated that the cost per monitoring a prisoner through this method is 70 percent lower than remanding that individual in prison."
  },
