    "md5": "5dfa6a3af09732d1f2712233a7095092",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1773,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s drug eradication campaign:  From rhetoric to real outcomes\n\nWith regards to drug rehabilitation, studies on selected programmes show that moderate success when psychological counselling, family involvement and structured aftercare are strong\n\nPublic Security Minister Ananda Wijepala\u2019s recent statements in Kurunegala have rightly focused national attention on the drug menace. The commitment to treat drug users as individuals requiring medical and rehabilitative intervention rather than automatic imprisonment, while reserving strict enforcement for traffickers and distributors, is a necessary and welcome correction. The readiness to bypass inactive police stations, the emphasis on not ignoring intelligence, and the establishment of national and district anti-drug councils alongside thousands of community-level public security committees signal political determination to break the decades-long nexus between narcotics, organised crime and political protection.\n\nThese are important signals. However, bold announcements and high-profile raids alone will not deliver a sustainable victory. It is easy to announce measures; it is far harder to implement them effectively. Much of what has been done in the past, and even some current operations,\u00a0 remains essentially reactive, responding after harm has already occurred. While it is better late than never to mount a serious national effort, these processes must now be made genuinely meaningful. They require clearly defined immediate, mid-term and long-term goals with measurable milestones and regular evaluation. If the campaign is to succeed, it must urgently strengthen four critical institutional pillars while placing prevention at the centre, driven by proactive and strategic intelligence."
