    "md5": "8a711b39a24b3fda4a1ec1c856dc9617",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1508,
    "chunk_text": "However, when a good intention is executed by departing from constitutional norms, the systemic damage far outweighs the short-term benefits. This can create a precedent that future administrations can exploit. Good intentions cannot cure a flawed process.\n\nThe public recognizes this hypocrisy. When politicians who previously weakened judicial independence suddenly present themselves as guardians of the Constitution, their arguments carry little weight. This creates a dangerous cynicism where citizens distrust both the government proposing the change and the opposition criticizing it.\n\nThe current administration has an opportunity to break this historical cycle and avoid setting precedents that it would object to if placed in opposition in the future. If a policy decision is made to raise the retirement age of judges, the state can still enact this extension provided it applies strictly to future appointees. True judicial efficiency is not achieved through tenure extensions for sitting judges, but through deliberate, foundational steps: building widespread public legal awareness, strengthening legal education, encouraging Public Interest Litigation (PIL), expanding the legal aid system, depoliticizing key state institutions including the police, and allocating significantly more funds toward these priorities in the national budget. By prioritizing these structural efforts, the government can modernize the legal system while safeguarding the foundational integrity of the Constitution."
  },
